UID:408fec919895ab9c@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260821T185317Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Expanding Affordable Housing in Maine
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nYou know\, we want every person at every stage of their life to be 
 confident they can rent or buy a home that's the right size for their fami
 ly and for their budget and be able to stay there as long as they want. No
  one should be afraid that a sudden rent increase will break their budget 
 or that someone will buy the land right out from under them and raise rent
 s.\nWhen Hidden Circle Mobile Home Park in Jay went up for sale\, that's e
 xactly what the residents were afraid of: someone hiking up the rent and e
 ventually pushing people out of their homes.\nAccording to the 2024 data\,
  nearly 10 percent of Maine people live in manufactured housing\, includin
 g mobile home communities. Forty-five thousand people in Maine live in a m
 obile home park. Out-of-state companies have started buying up some of Mai
 ne's mobile home parks and then significantly raising rents\, in some case
 s as high as 50 percent.\nAnd rent increases don't necessarily mean nicer 
 parks either\, no improvements necessarily. Corporations know that residen
 ts can't easily pick up their home and move it elsewhere\, no matter how m
 uch the company neglects the park. As one resident in Jay said\, "At one t
 ime\, the park used to look nice. Now they've stopped that\, and yet they 
 have increased our rent. If this continues to have higher lot rent payment
 s\, then I'm not going to be able to live where I am."\nThe buying up of m
 obile home parks by out-of-state entities is a nationwide trend\, but here
  in Maine\, we're doing something about it. Last year\, we enacted the "Ri
 ght of First Refusal" law\, which requires that residents of a mobile home
  park be given a chance to purchase their park if they can put together a 
 matching offer when it's put up for sale. That measure strengthened our 20
 23 "Opportunity to Purchase" law\, which required that residents of a mobi
 le home park be allowed to present a competitive offer to buy their park i
 f it's put up for sale.\nWell\, earlier\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mil
 ls/news/radio_address/expanding-affordable-housing-maine-2026-08-21
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/expanding-affor
 dable-housing-maine-2026-08-21
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
